# Line-ending policy — LF everywhere, on every platform.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. This repository was developed on macOS and later checked
# out on Windows, where Git's default `core.autocrlf=true` rewrote every text
# file to CRLF on checkout. That is not cosmetic here, it breaks three things:
#
#   1. `src/snapshot.py` SHA-256s params.yaml, dvc.yaml, requirements.txt and
#      requirements.lock.txt. CRLF changes those bytes, so `snapshot.py verify`
#      fails against a manifest produced anywhere else — release gate 2.
#   2. DVC hashes params.yaml as a stage dependency. CRLF makes `dvc status`
#      report every stage as changed, inviting a needless full `dvc repro`.
#   3. `scripts/*.sh` with CRLF fail under Git Bash and inside the Linux
#      container with `$'\r': command not found`.
#
# `eol=lf` is deliberate rather than `text=auto`: it normalises in the
# repository AND checks out LF, so a Windows working tree stays byte-identical
# to the macOS and CI ones. Windows tooling (PowerShell, VS Code, Python) all
# read LF without complaint.

*               text=auto eol=lf

# Executable POSIX scripts — must never gain CR, at any cost.
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.ps1           text eol=crlf

# Binary: never touched, and never diffed as text.
*.parquet       binary
*.pdf           binary
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.jpeg          binary
*.gif           binary
*.ico           binary
*.docx          binary
*.pptx          binary
*.xlsx          binary
*.zip           binary
*.woff          binary
*.woff2         binary
*.ttf           binary
*.otf           binary
